  "full_response": "### **Rate Limiting**\n**Rate limiting** is a technique used to control the amount of incoming or outgoing traffic to or from a network, server, or API within a specific time window. It helps prevent abuse, overuse of resources, denial-of-service (DoS) attacks, and ensures fair usage among clients.\n\n---\n\n### **Common Use Cases**\n1. **APIs** – Prevent excessive requests from a single client.\n2. **Web Servers** – Protect against brute-force attacks.\n3. **Microservices** – Ensure one service doesn’t overload another.\n4. **Cloud Services** – Enforce usage quotas (e.g., AWS, Stripe).\n5. **Login Systems** – Throttle failed login attempts.\n\n---\n\n### **Common Rate Limiting Algorithms**\n\n#### **1. Fixed Window (Static Window)**\n- **How it works**:\n  - Time is divided into fixed intervals (e.g., 1 minute).\n  - A counter tracks requests per window.\n  - If the counter exceeds the limit, requests are rejected.\n- **Pros**:\n  - Simple to implement.\n  - Low memory usage.\n- **Cons**:\n  - **Burst traffic** at window edges (e.g., 100 requests at 1:59 and 100 more at 2:00).\n  - Not precise for short bursts.\n- **Example**:\n  - Allow **100 requests per minute**.\n  - Reset counter at the start of each minute.\n\n#### **2. Sliding Window Log**\n- **How it works**:\n  - Tracks the **timestamp of each request** in a log (e.g., Redis Sorted Set).\n  - For each new request, old timestamps (outside the window) are removed.\n  - If remaining requests exceed the limit, the request is rejected.\n- **Pros**:\n  - Very accurate (no burst issues).\n- **Cons**:\n  - High memory usage (stores all timestamps).\n  - Slower due to log maintenance.\n- **Example**:\n  - Allow **100 requests per minute**.\n  - Store each request’s timestamp and reject if >100 in the last 60s.\n\n#### **3. Sliding Window Counter (Hybrid Approach)**\n- **How it works**:\n  - Combines **Fixed Window** and **Sliding Log** for efficiency.\n  - Uses **two fixed windows** (current and previous) and weights them.\n  - Formula:\n    ```\n   ",
